  "worked_solution": "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =0 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the fourth and first quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$",
